= Data structure — Simple =

A '''data structure''' is a way of organizing data so a computer can use it.

Different organizations make different jobs easier.

For example:

* an '''array''' stores items in an indexed sequence;
* a '''stack''' removes the most recently added item first;
* a '''queue''' removes the oldest waiting item first;
* a '''hash table''' uses keys to find values;
* a '''tree''' stores hierarchical relationships;
* a '''graph''' stores connections between things.

The choice of structure affects an [[Algorithm/Simple|algorithm]]. Searching, adding, deleting, or changing data may be fast with one structure and slow with another.

A data structure is also different from an '''abstract data type'''. An abstract data type describes what operations should do. A data structure describes how information can be organized to support those operations.

